.TH cp 1
.SH NAME
cp - copy a file.
.SH SYNOPSIS
cp [-fHiLpPRrv] [--] source... target
.SH DESCRIPTION
Copies one source file or recursive source directory to one target path, or multiple sources into an existing target directory.
.SH OPERANDS
source: input file path. target: output file path or target directory. A lone - is treated as an ordinary
pathname operand, not as standard input or an option.
.SH OPTIONS
-f forces overwrites without prompting, and removes an existing destination that cannot be opened for writing and
copies afresh. -H follows symbolic links named by source operands and copies the links inside a tree as links. -L
follows every symbolic link. -i prompts before overwriting each existing destination that is not a directory, inside a
recursive copy too; a directory that is merged into is never asked about. When -f and -i
are both supplied, the last one wins. -P copies symbolic links as symbolic links where hostkit supports link reading
and creation. -p preserves all twelve permission bits (set-user-ID, set-group-ID, and sticky included) and ownership where the
hostkit metadata service reports support, and preserves access and modification timestamps where the host timestamp
service supports it. An owner or group that cannot be kept, as for anyone but the superuser, is no failure; the copy
then keeps neither set-ID bit. -R and -r copy
directory trees recursively. -v writes deterministic source-to-target lines after
successful copies.
Options are recognized before the first source operand; later option-like words are path operands. The end-of-options
marker -- is accepted.
Project extensions --help, --version, and
--posix-tools-identify are recognized.
.SH STANDARD INPUT
Used only for -i overwrite responses.
.SH STANDARD OUTPUT
No data output on success unless -v is selected.
.SH STANDARD ERROR
Overwrite prompts and diagnostics for invalid usage and file failures.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 success, 1 operational failure, 2 invalid usage, 125 internal failure.
.SH BEHAVIORAL DETAILS
The command copies file contents through bounded internal buffers at the command boundary. Recursive
directory copying creates missing target directories and copies contained entries. Interactive overwrite prompts are
localized through messages, safely escape untrusted path text, and accept
an initial y or Y byte as confirmation; any other response, including end-of-input, skips that operand without
treating the skip as a copy failure. Without -R, symbolic links named by operands are followed unless -P is the
last selected traversal mode. The source is only read: no times are set on it.
Directory sources without -R or -r fail with a localized is a directory diagnostic. Recursive directory copies
fail immediately with a localized not a directory diagnostic when the destination path already exists as a
non-directory.
Copies that would write a source onto the same underlying destination file, including a destination hard link to the
source where hostkit reports identity, fail before writing. Recursive copies that would place the destination at or
below the source directory fail before creating the destination subtree; both paths are compared with their symbolic
links resolved, so a destination reached through a link to the source is refused too. When links are followed, a
directory that leads back to one being copied around it is reported as a file system loop, left out, and the copy goes
on with the rest, ending with status 1.
Without -R, a special file such as /dev/null is read for what it holds, and its copy is a regular file. Under -R,
FIFOs, character or block device nodes, and Unix-domain socket files are recreated through hostkit where the platform
exposes portable special-file metadata and permits creation.
Copying is done by the shared Posix_Tools.File_Operations.Copying, which explore uses too. A recursive copy visits
every entry of each directory, symbolic links whose targets are missing included: under -P they are copied as links,
and when links are followed they fail as unreadable files instead of being left out.
.SH LOCALE BEHAVIOR
Diagnostics and help are localized; copied data is not localized.
.SH IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED CHOICES
Multiple source operands require the target to be an existing directory. With -R and none of -H, -L, or -P,
symbolic links are copied as links, as under -P (POSIX leaves this choice to the implementation; BSD and GNU cp make
the same one). Ownership preservation with -p is attempted
only when source and target ownership metadata are both available, and it can still be refused by host permissions.
Special-file recreation is limited by host platform capabilities and ordinary filesystem permissions. Device-node
creation is commonly privileged. Windows does not have POSIX pathname sockets or device nodes, so those creation
operations are unavailable there.
.SH EXTENSIONS
--help, --version, --posix-tools-identify.
.SH EXAMPLES
cp input output
.SH CONFORMANCE STATUS
Conforming with extensions.
.SH KNOWN LIMITATIONS
No known limitation for the implemented V1 surface beyond host permission and platform capability limits documented
above.
.SH SEE ALSO
